Crusoe TSC does not halt in idle

Crusoe machines run on the acpi_pm clocksource since the 2006 timekeeping
rework: entering ACPI C2 marks the TSC unstable unless the CPU has
X86_FEATURE_NONSTOP_TSC, and tsc_check_state() only honours that for a few
vendors.  The TM5800 TSC keeps 800.0 MHz in C1/C2 and across LongRun
(measured against acpi_pm, and as a watchdog verified clocksource), so set
NONSTOP_TSC for Crusoe and honour it for Transmeta.  clock_gettime drops
from 2 us to 0.27 us (vDSO).

Signed-off-by: René Rebe <rene@exactco.de>

diff --git a/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/transmeta.c b/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/transmeta.c
index 3e04b5d01bf8..a3d2da8bd67c 100644
--- a/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/transmeta.c
+++ b/arch/x86/kernel/cpu/transmeta.c
@@ -96,6 +96,10 @@ static void init_transmeta(struct cpuinfo_x86 *c)
 	/* All Transmeta CPUs have a constant TSC */
 	set_cpu_cap(c, X86_FEATURE_CONSTANT_TSC);
 
+	/* Crusoe: TSC keeps its rate in C1/C2 and across LongRun (TM5800) */
+	if (c->x86 == 5)
+		set_cpu_cap(c, X86_FEATURE_NONSTOP_TSC);
+
 #ifdef CONFIG_SYSCTL
 	/*
 	 * randomize_va_space slows us down enormously;
diff --git a/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c b/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
index e113bcbbb882..f1ac91b53eff 100644
--- a/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
+++ b/drivers/acpi/processor_idle.c
@@ -198,6 +198,7 @@ static void tsc_check_state(int state)
 	case X86_VENDOR_INTEL:
 	case X86_VENDOR_CENTAUR:
 	case X86_VENDOR_ZHAOXIN:
+	case X86_VENDOR_TRANSMETA:
 		/*
 		 * AMD Fam10h TSC will tick in all
 		 * C/P/S0/S1 states when this bit is set.
